{"result":"{
  "id": "19132",
  "key": "SFCC-703",
  "summary": "PDP – Maximum Purchase Quantity Override by Customer Group and SKU",
  "description": "As a Site Merchandiser, I want to configure a maximum purchase quantity at the customer group and/or SKU level, so that I can restrict specific customers or specific products to a tighter purchase limit than the global default.\n\nCustomer group limits exist to further restrict certain shoppers — for example, customers identified as resellers may be placed in a customer group with a limit of 1, preventing bulk purchasing. The intent is always to restrict below the global default, not to grant higher limits. The most specific level takes priority: SKU limit → Customer Group limit → Global default.",
  "status": {
    "name": "To Do",
    "category": "To Do",
    "color": "blue-gray"
  },
  "assignee": {
    "display_name": "Unassigned"
  },
  "labels": [
    "Custom",
    "PRD-011",
    "Squad-A"
  ],
  "customfield_10231": {
    "value": "1. Given I am in Business Manager, when I open a product variant, then I can set a maximum purchase quantity at the variant (SKU) level without a code deploy.\n2. Given I am in Business Manager, when I open a Customer Group, then I can set a maximum purchase quantity on that group without a code deploy.\n3. Given a SKU has a maximum purchase quantity set, when a shopper adds that variant to bag, then the SKU limit is enforced regardless of the customer group or global default.\n4. Given a SKU has no maximum purchase quantity set, when a shopper adds that variant to bag, then the system falls back to the shopper's customer group limit.\n5. Given a SKU has no limit and the customer group has no limit, when a shopper adds that variant to bag, then the global default (SFCC-196) is applied.\n6. Given a shopper belongs to multiple customer groups each with different limits, when they add to bag, then the most restrictive (lowest) value across those groups applies.\n7. Given a SKU or customer group limit is blank, when the system resolves the limit, then it is treated as no override at this level and falls through to the next.\n8. Given any limit is reached, when the shopper attempts to add another unit, then the existing PDP inline messaging (SFCC-196) is triggered."
  },
  "customfield_10020": {
    "value": null
  }
}"}

